@h1 Self-organising documents h1@

The flagship idea behind Wit: model your structure as @strong data, once
strong@, then render many synchronized views from it. A table of contents, a
figure list, and a summary can all read the same collection — so reordering one
record re-renders every view, and nothing drifts out of sync.

@h2 The pattern h2@

Declare the structure as a collection of records, then iterate it into whatever
shape a given view needs. Here one @@@code #chapters code@@@ collection drives a
table of contents:

@@@pre
#chapters: [
  { number - I, title - The Lamp },
  { number - II, title - The Watch }
]

#tocrow ||number, title||
@li ::number::. ::title:: li@
tocrow#

@ul
(each @chapters as ch) @tocrow |number @ch.number| |title @ch.title| tocrow@ (end)
ul@
pre@@@

That renders a real list — @code I. The Lamp code@, @code II. The Watch code@
(verified). Add a chapter or reorder the collection and the list follows.

@h2 The techniques it stands on h2@

Everything here is a construct you already know, combined:

@ul
@li @strong Data as the source of truth strong@ — a
@@@code #chapters: [ … ] code@@@ collection (see
@a |href /docs/guides/data/| Data a@). li@
@li @strong Iteration strong@ — @@@code (each … as …) code@@@ over it (see
@a |href /docs/guides/iteration/| Iteration a@). li@
@li @strong Definitions with captures strong@ — a row template filled per item
(see @a |href /docs/guides/defining-nodes/| Defining nodes a@). li@
@li @strong Additive partials strong@ — for structure that grows across files
(see @a |href /docs/guides/additive-partials/| Additive partials a@). li@
ul@

Note the two ways data reaches a template: inside a loop, read a field with
data access — @@@code @ch.title code@@@ — and to feed a definition's capture,
pass it by pipes — @@@code @tocrow |title @ch.title| code@@@ — then read it as
@@@code ::title:: code@@@ inside the template.

@h2 What needs a renderer or a script h2@

Automatic @strong numbering strong@ and @strong back-references strong@ — "Figure
3", "as shown above" — are not core language features. Supply the numbers in the
data, or compute them in a @@@code <% %> code@@@ script (see
@a |href /docs/guides/derived-content/| Derived content a@). A custom node type
like a numbered figure box is a @strong renderer extension strong@, not core
behaviour.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Writing @@@code ::loopvar.field:: code@@@ in prose — use data access
@@@code @loopvar.field code@@@; the @@@code ::…:: code@@@ hole lives only in a
definition body. li@
@li Assuming a custom @@@code @node(type figure) code@@@ has built-in behaviour —
that is a renderer concern, not the core language. li@
ul@

